QUIC's assist thread must return the type the thread API says it does.

ossl_crypto_thread_native_start takes a CRYPTO_THREAD_ROUTINE, which
returns CRYPTO_THREAD_RETVAL -- a uint32_t. quic_thread_assist.c writes
its thread function as returning `unsigned int` instead, on the
assumption that uint32_t IS unsigned int.

On m68k it is not: this target's uint32_t is `long unsigned int`, so
the two function pointer types are incompatible and gcc refuses the
call outright (-Wincompatible-pointer-types, which OpenSSL builds with
as an error). The same assumption is what stops zstd's legacy decoders
compiling here; it is a property of the ABI, not of this build.

Using the typedef is correct everywhere and identical to what is there
now on every platform where uint32_t is unsigned int.

--- a/ssl/quic/quic_thread_assist.c
+++ b/ssl/quic/quic_thread_assist.c
@@ -16,7 +16,7 @@
 #if !defined(OPENSSL_NO_QUIC_THREAD_ASSIST)
 
 /* Main loop for the QUIC assist thread. */
-static unsigned int assist_thread_main(void *arg)
+static CRYPTO_THREAD_RETVAL assist_thread_main(void *arg)
 {
     QUIC_THREAD_ASSIST *qta = arg;
     CRYPTO_MUTEX *m = ossl_quic_channel_get_mutex(qta->ch);
